        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The title of the painting is first recorded in 1693, when it was listed in an inventory as Amor Divino e Amor Profano (Divine love and Profane love), and may not represent the original concept at all.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Sacred and Profane Love",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sacred_and_Profane_Love",
          "accessed": "2026-11-11",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Although \"much ink has been spilt by art historians attempting to decipher the iconography of the painting\", and some measure of consensus has been achieved, basic aspects of the intended meaning of the painting, including the identity of the central figures, remain disputed.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Sacred and Profane Love",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sacred_and_Profane_Love",
          "accessed": "2026-11-11",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "matters": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The painting is presumed to have been commissioned by Niccolò Aurelio, a secretary to the Venetian Council of Ten, whose coat of arms appears on the sarcophagus or fountain, to celebrate his marriage to a young widow, Laura Bagarotto.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Sacred and Profane Love",
          "publisher": "Wikipedia",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sacred_and_Profane_Love",
          "accessed": "2026-11-11",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "In her hair she wears myrtle, both a flower sacred to Venus and one worn by brides.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Sacred and Profane Love",
